"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,625\nThe Learning Brain. Series 4: Habits\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,435\nEpisode 12\n\n3\n00:00:07,515 --> 00:00:11,232\nProcrastination and the Pomodoro technique\n\n4\n00:00:14,032 --> 00:00:15,754\nYou plan to start the essay tonight.\n\n5\n00:00:16,084 --> 00:00:17,061\nThen tomorrow.\n\n6\n00:00:17,365 --> 00:00:19,123\nThen the night before it is due.\n\n7\n00:00:19,410 --> 00:00:20,892\nIf that sounds familiar,\n\n8\n00:00:20,912 --> 00:00:22,651\nyou are in large company.\n\n9\n00:00:22,821 --> 00:00:27,182\nA major review cites estimates that most college students procrastinate,\n\n10\n00:00:27,562 --> 00:00:32,321\nand that almost half do so consistently and in ways that cause problems.\n\n11\n00:00:32,727 --> 00:00:34,596\nIt is not simple laziness.\n\n12\n00:00:34,978 --> 00:00:38,682\nProcrastination means putting off something you intend to do,\n\n13\n00:00:38,945 --> 00:00:41,915\neven though you expect to be worse off for the delay.\n\n14\n00:00:42,085 --> 00:00:43,745\nIn a semester long study,\n\n15\n00:00:44,021 --> 00:00:48,545\nprocrastinators actually felt less stressed and healthier early in the term.\n\n16\n00:00:49,104 --> 00:00:52,491\nBut late in the term they reported more stress and illness,\n\n17\n00:00:52,742 --> 00:00:55,329\nand they earned lower grades on every assignment,\n\n18\n00:00:55,705 --> 00:00:59,280\naround three quarters of a letter grade lower on average.\n\n19\n00:00:59,450 --> 00:01:00,608\nCan deadlines help?\n\n20\n00:01:01,090 --> 00:01:07,096\nA well known 2002 study found that evenly spaced deadlines beat one final deadline.\n\n21\n00:01:07,649 --> 00:01:11,992\nBut a careful replication published this year did not find that advantage.\n\n22\n00:01:12,316 --> 00:01:14,864\nSo interim deadlines are worth trying,\n\n23\n00:01:15,132 --> 00:01:16,348\nnot a guarantee.\n\n24\n00:01:16,518 --> 00:01:18,441\nThen there is the Pomodoro technique: work in a short timed block,\n\n25\n00:01:18,850 --> 00:01:20,747\nthen take a planned break.\n\n26\n00:01:21,003 --> 00:01:22,373\nIn a Dutch study of eighty seven university students,\n\n27\n00:01:22,950 --> 00:01:26,077\nthose given fixed breaks,\n\n28\n00:01:26,279 --> 00:01:30,286\nsuch as six minutes after every twenty four,\n\n29\n00:01:30,475 --> 00:01:32,578\nfelt less tired and distracted,\n\n30\n00:01:32,848 --> 00:01:36,367\nand more motivated, than students who chose their own breaks.\n\n31\n00:01:36,816 --> 00:01:39,296\nThey got the same work done in less time.\n\n32\n00:01:39,466 --> 00:01:41,924\nA later study found smaller differences.\n\n33\n00:01:42,285 --> 00:01:46,594\nSo the exact numbers matter less than deciding your breaks before you start.\n\n34\n00:01:46,764 --> 00:01:47,731\nSome caution.\n\n35\n00:01:48,171 --> 00:01:49,656\nThese are short studies,\n\n36\n00:01:49,676 --> 00:01:52,953\nand much procrastination research is correlational.\n\n37\n00:01:53,353 --> 00:01:56,901\nAnd if avoidance is tied to anxiety or low mood,\n\n38\n00:01:56,916 --> 00:01:57,849\ntalk to someone.\n\n39\n00:01:58,242 --> 00:02:00,215\nIt is not just a time problem.\n\n40\n00:02:00,385 --> 00:02:01,261\nTry this.\n\n41\n00:02:01,585 --> 00:02:05,463\nStart with one twenty five minute block on the task you are avoiding.\n\n42\n00:02:05,817 --> 00:02:07,686\nDecide your breaks before you begin,\n\n43\n00:02:07,706 --> 00:02:08,922\nand keep them short.\n\n44\n00:02:09,359 --> 00:02:12,030\nSplit big assignments into dated steps.\n\n45\n00:02:12,278 --> 00:02:14,279\nAnd notice the relief once you start.\n\n46\n00:02:14,651 --> 00:02:16,438\nThat is the feeling to remember.\n\n47\n00:02:16,608 --> 00:02:18,019\nDon't wait to feel ready.\n\n48\n00:02:18,110 --> 00:02:19,299\nStart the timer.\n\n"
